# rclone filter rules for the bisync pair, one per line.
# Syntax: https://rclone.org/filtering/
#   - pattern    exclude
#   + pattern    include
# A pattern starting with "/" is anchored to the sync root; otherwise it matches
# at any depth.
#
# Everything listed here is ignored on BOTH sides, so those files simply stay as
# they are locally and in the cloud.
#
# Do NOT put quotes around a pattern. rclone reads this file itself; a shell is
# never involved, so '- *.tmp' is a pattern that starts with an apostrophe and
# matches nothing. That mistake shipped here once and made half of these rules
# inert.
#
# NOTE: changing this file requires a one-off baseline rebuild:
#     onedrive-sync --resync

# --- files OneDrive and its own client will not sync --------------------------
# Office writes "~$document.docx" next to an open document and deletes it on
# close. Microsoft reserves names starting with ~$, and syncing a file that is
# being written produces conflicts for nothing.
- ~$*
# Microsoft reserves the name .lock. Nothing legitimate is called that.
- .lock

# --- caches / indexes that are machine-specific and get regenerated -----------
# (example: the .rag vector index of an Obsidian RAG plugin, often hundreds of MB)
- /.rag/**

# --- per-machine UI state ----------------------------------------------------
# Workspace layout flip-flopping between two machines is pure noise.
- /.obsidian/workspace.json
- /.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json

# --- OS junk -----------------------------------------------------------------
- .DS_Store
- Thumbs.db
- desktop.ini
# A bare directory name does not prune anything: rclone still walks into a
# directory it excludes, so the contents need the /** as well.
- .Trash-*/**
- *~
- *.swp
- *.tmp
- *.partial

# --- language caches ---------------------------------------------------------
- **/__pycache__/**
- *.pyc
